course: dictionary
lesson: 5978
title: A word for help
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Mayday!» is a word you may hear in stories about planes and ships. It is a call for help when someone is in great danger, such as an engine failure or a boat sinking. The context tells you this: the pilot shouts it when something has gone very badly wrong.
explain: The word is said three times on the radio, «Mayday, mayday, mayday», so everyone listening knows it is a real emergency. Informally, people also use it for any urgent call for help, but the first meaning is about life-or-death danger.
explain: Do not mix it up with May Day, which has two words and capital letters. That is the first day of May, a spring festival and a workers' holiday. Look at the capital letters and the rest of the sentence to tell which one is meant.
fact: Mayday is a call for help used by ships and aircraft in great danger.
fact: You say it three times on the radio: «Mayday, mayday, mayday».
fact: «Pan-pan» is a smaller urgent call, used when there is no immediate danger to life.
fact: Mayday is only for real emergencies, and using it falsely is a crime.
fact: Mayday comes from French «m'aider», meaning «help me», from «venez m'aider», «come and help me». It was chosen in the 1920s.
exercise: «The pilot shouted "Mayday!" as the engine failed.» What does Mayday show? | A happy shout at a party | A French lesson | *A call for help in great danger | The name of a ship
exercise: Which meaning is May Day, the one with two words and capital letters? | The name of a pilot | A crime | A call for help on the radio | *The first day of May, a spring festival
exercise: «Mayday» comes from «m'aider», meaning «help me». Which language is it from? | *French | German | Italian | Spanish
test: A boat is sinking, and the crew send out a mayday. Why? | *They are in serious danger and need help | They are celebrating May Day | They want a picnic | They are testing the radio
test: What is the smaller urgent call used when there is no immediate danger to life? | May Day | Mayday | *Pan-pan | RSVP
exam: Why must you never send a mayday falsely? | *It is a crime, because it is only for real emergencies | It is only a rude word | It is the same as May Day | It makes the radio louder
